    {
      "id": "SFPL",
      "code_hash": "d04cc8644f6bc060efc4a0a9658c6968",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><div class=\"language-python codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">batch</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">torch</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">3.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">2.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">4.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">3.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">2.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">1.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">5.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">4.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n<span class=\"p\">])</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>      <span class=\"c1\"># first house</span>\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[:,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># second feature (all houses)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># first feature of third house</span>\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[:</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>     <span class=\"c1\"># first two houses</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"language-python codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>    <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"mf\">3.</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">2.</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[:,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([</span><span class=\"mf\">2.</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">3.</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">1.</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">4.</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"mf\">2.0</span>\n<span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"p\">[:</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">]</span>   <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([[</span><span class=\"mf\">3.</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">2.</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n        <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">4.</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">3.</span><span class=\"p\">]])</span>\n</code></pre></div></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "BYtC",
      "code_hash": "19340cdc194ab33425f9fe69ba2f1751",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"the-aha-moment-why-we-need\">The \"Aha!\" Moment: Why We Need <code>@</code></h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Suppose we have our batch of 4 houses, each with 2 features. We also have a set of \"weights\" that tell us how important each feature is. We want to multiply our houses by our weights to get a prediction.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "RGSE",
      "code_hash": "083cbbe70802e326dc8e58fb2c295b5c",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": ""
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "Kclp",
      "code_hash": "7d6069faac90764c7bce7b887970e462",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><span class=\"paragraph\">If we try to use standard multiplication <code>*</code>, PyTorch will try to match up the numbers element by element. </span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We don't want element-wise multiplication. We want to apply our <code>(2, 1)</code> weights to <em>every single row</em> of our <code>(4, 2)</code> batch simultaneously.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">We need <strong>Matrix Multiplication</strong>, represented in PyTorch by the <code>@</code> symbol.</span>\n<div class=\"language-python codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">batch</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">torch</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">3.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">2.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">4.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">3.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">2.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">1.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">5.0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mf\">4.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n<span class=\"p\">])</span> <span class=\"c1\"># shape: (4, 2)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">weights</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">torch</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">50.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n    <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">25.0</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n<span class=\"p\">])</span> <span class=\"c1\"># shape: (2, 1)</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\"># Matrix Multiplication</span>\n<span class=\"n\">predictions</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">batch</span> <span class=\"o\">@</span> <span class=\"n\">weights</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"language-python codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">batch</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">shape</span>   <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">weights</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">shape</span> <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"n\">predictions</span>   <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"n\">tensor</span><span class=\"p\">([[</span><span class=\"mf\">200.</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n        <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">275.</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n        <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">125.</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n        <span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">350.</span><span class=\"p\">]])</span>\n<span class=\"n\">predictions</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">shape</span> <span class=\"o\">-&gt;</span> <span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Look at what happens to the shapes:</span>\n<marimo-tex class=\"arithmatex\">||[\n(4, 2) @ (2, 1) \nightarrow (4, 1)\n||]</marimo-tex><span class=\"paragraph\">The inner dimensions (<code>2</code> and <code>2</code>) snap together and collapse. We are left with a shape of <code>(4, 1)</code>: exactly 4 predictions, one for each house. We just processed the entire batch in a single operation, without a single <code>for</code> loop.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
    {
      "id": "emfo",
      "code_hash": "d5cd303176bb23cebcce9a4083de12a0",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"the-reality-check-adapting-shapes\">The Reality Check: Adapting Shapes</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">In the real world, your data rarely arrives in the exact perfect shape for matrix multiplication. Sometimes the numbers are right, but the Lego blocks won't snap together.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">This is why PyTorch gives us tools to bend and adapt our tensors:</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
